WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.000
-Guys, this is exciting.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.167
I'm about to bring out a
very special guest.

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:04.434
He's a groundbreaking
digital artist

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:07.434
whose works have
sold for over $100 million.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:10.100
He's currently working on
a project called "Everydays,"

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.200
where he's been making one piece
of digital art every single day.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.234
And yesterday marked
the 15th anniversary

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:16.434
of when he first started.

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:18.100
Here to talk about it,
give it up for Beeple,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.133
ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:23.200
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.167
It's nice to see you. Beeple.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:26.901
-Hey.
-Thank you for being here.

00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:28.033
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:30.567
-I want to talk to you about
"Everydays,"

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:31.901
this project "Everydays."

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:34.601
Yesterday was the
15th anniversary of it

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.334
to when you first started it.

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:40.067
What was your goal when you
started this 15 years ago?

00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:42.834
-Yeah, it started
on May 1, 2007.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.667
So it's been a sec here.
But I just wanted to get better.

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.267
And I started out
doing drawings,

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.300
and they were not very good.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.701
And I wanted to
get better at it,

00:00:50.701 --> 00:00:52.834
and that's why
I'm still doing it today.

00:00:52.834 --> 00:00:54.200
I still really
want to get better.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.868
-We have your first one.
-Alright, okay.

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:57.400
-This is your first one
that you've done there.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.734
Yeah, that's "Uber Jay."
-See? There you go.

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:01.501
I mean, you can see
why I would want to improve.

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:03.501
-Yeah. I think that's fine.

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:04.834
I mean, and then, that's that,

00:01:04.834 --> 00:01:06.968
but then here's the one
you did yesterday.

00:01:06.968 --> 00:01:08.434
Ooh.
-Slightly better.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:09.567
-Well, see?

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:10.634
Look at the reaction
from the audience.

00:01:10.634 --> 00:01:13.200
-Wow!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.901
You go from "Uber Jay" --

00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:17.400
Uber Jay, by the way,
was that your uncle?

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.067
-Uncle Jim, yeah.
He's gotten some airtime here.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.634
-Yeah, he has some airtime here.

00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:23.901
But, so, yeah,
you have been getting better

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:25.968
and doing crazier stuff.

00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:27.801
Well, you haven't made
today's drawing yet.

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:29.200
-I have not.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.467
-And this is an idea that we had
last time we saw each other,

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:32.901
and I go,
"Can we pull this off?"

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.667
Tonight, you're going to create
it for us during the show.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.167
-Yep.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:41.534
-Now, how does this work?

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.234
How can we help?
Do you have a screen?

00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:45.734
What do you have? A light pen?
Do you have a...

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:47.400
-So, I do have
a computer back there.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.067
And I start from scratch
each day.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.400
But what I do have is, I have a
3-D model of your head

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.000
that was made to me
by my buddy Raf, and --

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.934
-Do I know Raf?
-Raf Grassetti.

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.267
-Is Raf a fan of mine?
-He's a fan. He's a fan.

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:01.634
-Okay, good.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.934
And, so, we've got a 3-D model
and we've got all these

00:02:03.934 --> 00:02:06.601
different sort of, like, objects
that we can use to kind of,

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:08.167
like, fill out the scene.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.234
So it's kind of like
having a bunch of toys

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:12.767
that we can kind of, like,
place to, like, make a picture.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:15.534
So, I was actually looking for
you to kind of, like,

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:18.567
come up with some sort of, like,
suggestions of what

00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:20.334
you think
the picture should be today.

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:24.467
-Well, it's "The Tonight Show."
We make a lot of people laugh.

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:27.767
We -- Also, a lot of people
fall asleep to our show.

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:32.434
-Okay. We'll see if we can
put that into the picture.

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:35.133
-Our logo is the moon,
the "Tonight Show" moon.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:37.334
So you could do something
on the moon, maybe.

00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:41.767
-Moon, people sleeping,
laughing.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:42.901
-That kind of sums up our show.
-Your face.

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:43.934
-Yeah.
-Your face.

00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:45.067
-My face.

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.467
Yeah, it kind of
sums up my face, yeah.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.234
Yeah, or anything like that.
And, also, it's spring.

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:51.801
Anything else?
-Flowers.

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:53.534
-Flowers?
-Shout out things.

00:02:53.534 --> 00:02:55.434
-Flowers!
-Flowers. Okay.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.067
[ Indistinct shouting ]

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:58.133
-Did you say "mace"?

00:02:58.133 --> 00:03:00.834
No, I don't know -- Mace?
What is that?

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:02.534
-The Roots!
-Jester, jester.

00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:03.701
-A jester?
-Jester, okay.

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:05.801
-Ooh, interesting.
-Cherry blossoms, jester.

00:03:05.801 --> 00:03:07.334
We got a couple things
to work with.

00:03:07.334 --> 00:03:08.434
-Yeah, we got some things
to work with here.

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:09.868
-A couple things.
-Alright, perfect.

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:13.234
Now, so, when you do this,
how long does it normally take?

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.467
-It usually takes
about two hours.

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:16.467
-Okay, we don't have
that tonight.

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:17.567
-We do not have two hours.
-No.

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:18.734
-We do not have two hours.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:20.300
-So you're just gonna go
and just get in there.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.367
And we're going to be
checking in.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.968
Okay, you have a room
with a thing.

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.767
If you need anything from me,
let me know, okay, bud?

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:26.501
-Okay, we got it?
-Alright, Beeple right there.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:27.901
Good luck.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:31.601
Alright, Beeple's going off.
Go, go, go, go!

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:34.033
We're going to check in with him
throughout the show

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:35.534
and see how he's doing.

00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:36.868
And then,
at the end of the show,

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:39.701
we're going to unveil Beeple's
latest "Everydays" piece.

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:41.534
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:43.467
Can't wait to see
what he comes up with.

00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:44.634
♪♪

00:03:44.634 --> 00:03:46.534
-♪ Unh ♪

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:48.300
♪ Legendary, hard-workin' ♪

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.200
♪ Dance ♪

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.901
♪♪

00:03:57.901 --> 00:03:59.100
-Welcome back.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.167
Should we check in with
Beeple really quick

00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:02.501
and just see -- Is he at work?

00:04:02.501 --> 00:04:05.300
Beeple, are you -- Can you --
Yeah, you're at work.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.834
Can you give us taste
of what it is or not really?

00:04:08.834 --> 00:04:12.968
-Uh, I an give you a taste.
I don't if you want a taste --

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:15.634
-Alright, maybe I don't want
a taste right now.

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:17.033
Alright, hold on. Let's just go.
I'll start the show.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:18.067
Here we go.
♪♪

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:38.167
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:39.601
Welcome back, everybody.

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:41.934
We're joined by one of the most
innovative digital artists

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:43.467
out there, Beeple, right here.

00:04:43.467 --> 00:04:46.267
Now, during the show,

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:49.667
you made a brand-new piece
of digital art for us.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.567
How did it go, by the way?
-Well...

00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:52.767
-Uh-oh.

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:54.434
-This is usually
a little quicker

00:04:54.434 --> 00:04:55.901
than I'm used to working.

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:58.467
I haven't done that many pieces
on "The Tonight Show,"

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:00.167
so this is definitely
a little new for me.

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:02.701
-Yeah, well, I don't think
anyone's ever done this

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:04.801
on television before,
to make a brand-new

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:07.601
piece of art during the show,
so thank you for doing this.

00:05:07.601 --> 00:05:10.334
Now, what are you going to
call this piece?

00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:13.200
-Um...I don't know.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.334
I'm going to be honest.

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:16.000
I just made it literally
like 10 minutes ago.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.200
-Oh, yeah.
So, think about the name.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.734
-We'll think about the name.
-Alright, yeah.

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:21.400
Now, before we pull this off
and reveal,

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.100
are we going to be friends
after this?

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.834
-I think we will,
but I would like to point out,

00:05:26.834 --> 00:05:30.133
you were the one that said
people sleep through the show.

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:31.567
I did not say that.

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:34.033
-No, no, eventually,
they go to sleep.

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:37.067
-Okay, okay.
Po-tay-to, po-tah-to.

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:39.267
It's what it is.
-Eventually, yeah.

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.367
Alright, here we go.
[ Drumroll ]

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:43.400
It is time for the big reveal.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.834
In honor of the 15th anniversary
of his "Everydays" series,

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:52.200
here's Beeple's latest piece,
titled -- I don't know --

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.300
"Late Night Sleep."

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.734
I have no idea.

00:05:54.734 --> 00:05:56.734
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:06:00.334 --> 00:06:04.167
-You got the people
sleeping in there.

00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:08.734
-Hopefully they're sleeping.
Oh, my God. A giant head.

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:09.801
Dude, this is wild.

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.200
Well, you've got the moon
in there, though.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.567
-We got the moon.
We got the cherry blossoms.

00:06:12.567 --> 00:06:15.067
-You got the cherry blossoms.
-People sleeping.

00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:17.334
-This is what people wanted.
-You're smiling.

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:20.367
-I am smiling. I love this.
Beeple, this is a work of art.

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:21.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:25.968
Congratulations.

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:29.033
I can't wait to see what you do
in the next 15 years.

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:30.400
There's Beeple right there.
Beeple.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.834
-Thank you, guys. Thank you.

00:06:31.834 --> 00:06:35.367
-My thanks to Norman Reedus,
Paula Pell, The Linda Lindas.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:38.467
Beeple once again. I love it.

